# ── libccid >= 1.5.3: NO BREW ROUTE, AND NONE IS NEEDED (2026-08-31) ──────────
# NOT a row. A measured absence, recorded so the next agent does not re-propose
# the install line that does not resolve. Prompted by Ani's finding that a
# CardContact SmartCard-HSM in Identiv's uTrust Token Flex housing is attached to
# the maintainer's host and invisible to the smartcard stack.
#
# THE DIAGNOSIS IS CONFIRMED, independently, from the host itself:
#   ioreg -r -c IOUSBHostDevice -l -n "uTrust Token Flex"
#     idVendor 1254 (0x04E6 Identiv) · idProduct 22566 (0x5826) · serial 5599...
#     "CCID Interface@0" bInterfaceClass 11 · UsbExclusiveOwner = usbsmartcardreaderd
#   /usr/libexec/SmartCardServices/drivers/ifd-ccid.bundle -- CFBundleShortVersionString 1.5.1
#     556 ifdProductID entries · 0x5816 and 0x5817 PRESENT · 0x5826 ABSENT
#     (absent for every vendor, not just 0x04E6)
# libccid added the Token Flex in 1.5.3; Apple ships 1.5.1. The gap is a stale
# VID/PID table, and nothing else. The device is enumerated and its CCID
# interface is claimed by Apple's daemon -- so this is NOT a missing-daemon
# problem, which is exactly why installing one would be the wrong move.
#
# THE PROPOSED REMEDY DOES NOT RESOLVE. `brew install ccid` was checked against
# the Homebrew API rather than recalled, with a control that passes:
#   https://formulae.brew.sh/api/formula/ccid.json    404  (GitHub Pages HTML)
#   https://formulae.brew.sh/api/cask/ccid.json       404
#   https://formulae.brew.sh/api/formula/libccid.json 404
#   https://formulae.brew.sh/api/formula.json         200 -- 8,580 formulae parsed;
#     the ONLY ccid/pcsc/smartcard name match is `pcsc-lite`
#   brew info --formula ccid  -> Error: No available formula ... Did you mean cidr?
#   brew info --formula opensc -> resolves (0.27.1)   <- the control
# This is the `yubihsm-shell` lesson from the block above, in its other polarity:
# there the 404s were read as "no package" when a CASK existed; here there is no
# formula, no cask, and no alternate spelling. `pcsc-lite` is not a substitute --
# it is the daemon, not the driver, and macOS already runs PC/SC.
#
# NOTE the 404 returns rc=0 with an HTML body. Read the status code, not $?.
#
# NOR IS THE HOST-CONFIG HALF SAFE ON ITS OWN. `sudo defaults write
# /Library/Preferences/com.apple.security.smartcard useIFDCCID -bool yes` is a
# real, live mechanism -- `strings` on
# /System/Library/CryptoTokenKit/usbsmartcardreaderd.slotd/.../usbsmartcardreaderd
# carries `useIFDCCID`, `com.apple.security.smartcard`, the built-in bundle path,
# AND `file:///usr/local/libexec/SmartCardServices/drivers/`. That pref points the
# daemon at the third-party drop-in directory. With no brew formula there is
# nothing to put in that directory, so setting it today aims a working stack at an
# empty dir -- and the plausible failure is not "no change", it is the YubiKey and
# YubiHSM paths that currently work breaking. Precondition first, pref second.
# (The domain does not exist on the maintainer's host as of 2026-08-31 -- checked.)
#
# THE LINUX ASYMMETRY IS NOT SYMMETRIC EITHER, and manifests/apt should not be
# read as covering this. It declares `pcscd`, whose control fields are
# `Depends: libccid | pcsc-ifd-handler`, so libccid arrives transitively -- but at
# the release's version, and that is where the coverage splits. Measured against
# the Ubuntu indices 2026-08-31:
#   dists/noble/universe/binary-amd64  libccid 1.5.5-1  (64,755 pkgs)  -> COVERS 0x5826
#   dists/jammy/universe/binary-amd64  libccid 1.5.0-2  (58,923 pkgs)  -> DOES NOT
#   dists/{noble,jammy}/main           libccid ABSENT
# So: noble covers this token, jammy does not, macOS does not. No apt row is
# needed or wanted (a hard-pinned libccid row would fight pcscd's own dependency).
#
# AND NO VERSION FLOOR EITHER -- asked 2026-08-31 and answered no, with reasons,
# because "a transitive dependency at an unpinned version" is otherwise exactly the
# shape that deserves one:
#   1. A `libccid (>= 1.5.3)` row is UNSATISFIABLE on jammy, which we support and
#      test (docker-ubuntu-jammy-install-sh-test.yml). It would convert a
#      hypothetical device-support gap into a CERTAIN install failure on a live
#      lane. That is a strictly worse trade than the gap.
#   2. It is the wrong KIND of check. Whether a reader works is a runtime property
#      of a host that has the device; a package version is a proxy for it, and the
#      macOS result above is precisely a case where the proxy said NO and the
#      property was YES. Assert the property, not the proxy.
#   3. The instrument that settles it already exists and is now installed:
#      `opensc-tool --list-readers` on a Linux host with the token attached, with a
#      known-good CCID device as the positive control. UNMEASURED -- no such host
#      exists in this fleet today. Named so it is a runnable experiment rather than
#      an assumption, in either direction.
# Note the macOS result does NOT transfer by analogy. macOS and Windows match on
# the USB CCID interface class; Linux's pcsc-lite loads an ifdhandler and libccid
# keeps a VID/PID list it extends every release -- which is itself evidence the
# Linux table is load-bearing where the macOS one was not. Evidence, not proof:
# see (3).
#
# WINDOWS: nothing to add. Windows drives CCID through its in-box usbccid class
# driver, which matches on the USB smartcard class rather than a shipped VID/PID
# table, so there is no table to be stale. A row here would no-op -- the vacuity
# class the pam-reattach block below names.
#
# ══ REFUTED, SAME DAY, BY MEASUREMENT. THE READER WORKS. ═════════════════════
# Everything above this line is CONFIRMED and stays. The paragraph that used to
# stand here did not: it claimed the stale table BLOCKED the secp256k1 falsifier,
# and it was wrong. `opensc` was installed (it was declared in this manifest and
# ABSENT on the host -- see the block below) and the reader enumerated at once:
#
#   $ opensc-tool --list-readers
#   Nr.  Card  Features  Name
#   0    Yes             Yubico YubiKey FIDO+CCID
#   1    Yes             Identiv uTrust Token Flex        <- card present, rc=0
#
# No `ccid` installed. No `useIFDCCID` set. `/usr/local/libexec/SmartCardServices/
# drivers/` still does not exist. So NOTHING IS BLOCKED and NOTHING IS TO BE
# INSTALLED. Recorded as measured-unnecessary rather than deleted, so the install
# line does not get re-proposed in six weeks.
#
# HYPOTHESIS, labelled as one: macOS most likely matches a standards-compliant
# reader on the USB CCID INTERFACE CLASS (0x0B) and consults the VID/PID table for
# quirks and non-compliant devices -- so a compliant reader is picked up
# generically and a missing table row costs nothing. NOT MEASURED. What is
# measured is only that it works.
#
# DO NOT RUN the `defaults write ... useIFDCCID -bool yes` line. It was already
# unsafe (it aims the daemon at a directory that does not exist); it is now also
# pointless, and the devices it could break include a live YubiHSM. There is no
# ordering that makes it safe because there is no first half to order it after.
#
# ── THE REUSABLE PART: A NEGATIVE FROM THE WRONG INSTRUMENT IS `unknown` ──────
# The chain broke at the conclusion, not at any observation. Every fact above was
# right. The inference that turned them into "the reader does not work" came from
# ONE probe:
#
#   security list-smartcards   ->   (empty)
#
# which lists CRYPTOTOKENKIT tokens -- applets with a CTK driver -- and not PC/SC
# readers. An empty CTK list never meant "no reader". It meant "no CTK driver for
# this applet", which is true, expected, and irrelevant. The instrument answered a
# different question and its silence was read as an answer to ours.
#
# THE CONTROL WAS AVAILABLE THE ENTIRE TIME and nobody ran it. Two other CCID
# devices were attached to the same host (YubiKey 0x1050:0x0406, YubiHSM
# 0x1050:0x0030). `security list-smartcards` is empty with those attached too --
# so a single run with a known-good device would have shown the instrument was
# measuring something else, immediately, before a week of driver archaeology.
#
# This block already knew the discipline and applied it to the OTHER half: the
# 404s above are trusted precisely BECAUSE `brew info --formula opensc` returns a
# 200 on the same transport. A control was demanded of the packaging question and
# not of the hardware question. That asymmetry, not the libccid table, is what
# cost the week.
#
#   Rule: a negative result is `unknown` until a POSITIVE CONTROL on the same
#   instrument has produced a positive. Sibling instances already on file --
#   `system_profiler SPUSBDataType` returns zero lines on this host (a check that
#   does not run, not an empty bus); a required check that never ran contributes
#   zero to a failure count and reads as green; exit code 2 is a check that never
#   ran, not one that failed.
#
# WHAT ACTUALLY BLOCKED THE FALSIFIER: `opensc` was declared here and never
# installed on any host. The falsifier in
# docs/research/2026-08-22-smartcard-hsm-secp256k1-is-unconfirmed-*.md was
# runnable the moment the hardware was attached; the tool that runs it was a line
# of text in this file and nothing else. That is a PACKAGING failure of exactly
